        "notes": "Read the entire 1412-line file. No repo file was created, modified, or deleted: mtimes on scripts/compile-corpus-index.py (2026-07-20 09:03:33) and corpus/index/compiled-index.json (2026-07-20 09:03:58) are unchanged and 8 days old; both were already 'M' in the pre-existing git status. scripts/compile-corpus-index.py was never executed as a script — I imported it read-only (main() is __main__-guarded, so no write path ran) and called the collect_*/parse_* functions in memory. Throwaway analysis scripts live outside the repo in the session scratchpad.\n\nCLAIM 9 — filename patterns. The handoff says four; there are FIVE distinct filename patterns, and only TWO are actual .glob() calls. Verbatim: (glob) \"*-ontology.md\" at scripts/compile-corpus-index.py:1208, Aristotle flat dir only; (glob) \"*-cross-pipeline-hooks.md\" at :1244, Aristotle dir only; (literal) \"book-level-ontology.md\" at :1199 and :1221; (literal) \"cluster-ontology.md\" at :1223, tried only as a fallback when book-level-ontology.md is absent; (literal) \"tension-edges.json\" at :1267. A sixth string, \"compiled-index.json\" at :32, is the output, not an input.\n\nCLAIM 10 — output shape. compiled-index.json has one scalar plus four top-level arrays, built at scripts/compile-corpus-index.py:1374-1380 and written at :1384-1387. On-disk record counts (verified against the file, and reproduced exactly by an in-memory recompile): builtAt = \"2026-07-20T16:03:58.499584+00:00\" (scalar); ontologyNodes = 701 records (from 771 raw, 70 merged away); crossPipelineHooks = 72 records; tensionEdges = 64 records (from 119 raw, 55 silently dropped); canonicalTerms = 1722 strings.\n\nEXIT-CODE DISCIPLINE — none at all. No sys.exit, no SystemExit, no return code, no --strict, no warning counter, no threshold, no post-write validation. main() at :1361 returns None and the module always exits 0; the sole nonzero path is an uncaught traceback, and nothing in the collect_* layer is wrapped in try/except.\n\nLOGGING INFRASTRUCTURE it could hook into — essentially none, but the seam is trivial. The whole apparatus is one four-line helper: `def warn(msg: str) -> None: print(f\"WARNING: {msg}\", file=sys.stderr)` at :153-154. The `logging` module is not imported (imports are json, re, sys, datetime, pathlib at :20-24). There are exactly 11 warn() callsites (:162, :287, :319, :549, :622, :1093, :1104, :1113, :1188, :1193, :1205, :1229, :1241 — all in already-guarded paths that do not fire on the current corpus), plus one inconsistent `print(\"  WARNING: ...\")` to STDOUT at :1356. Because every diagnostic funnels through the single warn() function, adding a module-level counter there — plus `sys.exit(1)` when it is nonzero — is a ~5-line change that would convert all 17 silent-skip sites into a hard failure, provided each site is first given a warn() call. That is the highest-leverage fix in the file.\n\nONE CORRECTION WORTH CARRYING FORWARD: the handoff's phrase \"malformed input is indistinguishable from absent input\" is exactly right, but it understates the situation. On the corpus as it stands today, malformed input is also indistinguishable from a SUCCESSFUL compile — the run prints a tidy summary, exits 0, and emits not one warning, while 55 tension records and 3 whole clusters go missing."
